A strong counter-example to your last statement about off-diagonal elements in $\\Sigma^{-1}$ is afforded by the simplest nontrivial example in two dimensions, $\\Sigma^{-1}=\\left(
\\begin{array}{cc}
 \\frac{1}{1-\\rho ^2} & -\\frac{\\rho }{1-\\rho ^2} \\\\
 -\\frac{\\rho }{1-\\rho ^2} & \\frac{1}{1-\\rho ^2} \\\\
\\end{array}
\\right).$  The larger off-diagonal values correspond to *more* extreme values of the correlation coefficient $\\rho,$ which is the opposite of what you appear to be saying.

